    "narrative": "The Zurich Zebras enter today\u2019s slate with **Yordan Alvarez** pacing the offense as our clear elite option at 87.42. Facing Mikolas with an inviting 4.59 xERA, **Yordan Alvarez** benefits from a massive 14% skill-gap advantage, combined with a 7% platoon boost that cements him as our primary anchor. **Kyle Schwarber** (69.59) and **Trea Turner** (68.53) follow close behind; both benefit significantly from the matchup against Cameron. **Trea Turner** remains the linchpin for our SB-scarcity strategy, and despite a 4% penalty for Cameron\u2019s hold ability, his leadoff placement and favorable park factors make him a mandatory start for stolen base potential. **Christian Yelich** rounds out our high-leverage group with a 63.53 score, driven primarily by a 13% dynamic platoon edge against May.\n\nOur situational focus today centers on **Casey Schmitt** (54.41) and **Kazuma Okamoto** (53.23), both of whom are projected as marginal-to-strong starts despite some SP pressure. **Casey Schmitt** receives an unconventional boost due to Gausman\u2019s slow delivery, which the model identifies as a prime opportunity to chase SB efficiency, even against a tough arm. Meanwhile, the decision to start **Nelson Velazquez** (47.37) over the remainder of the bench was narrow; while his score is modest due to a difficult matchup against the lefty Drohan, his elite xwOBA of 0.421 keeps his floor elevated compared to the inactive status of players like **Pete Crow-Armstrong**, **Caleb Durbin**, **Colt Keith**, and **Hyeseong Kim**. \n\nWe must remain vigilant regarding the 34% rain risk surrounding **Carson Benge** (57.02). While his projected score is solid due to the matchup against L\u00f3pez, any weather delay could force a late adjustment; managers should monitor active rosters closely if the forecast degrades. While the bench is largely stagnant today due to scheduling gaps for the likes of **Lars Nootbaar**, **Chase Meidroth**, and **Gary Sanchez**, the current lineup optimizes our ceiling in the SB and power categories effectively. We are avoiding the risks associated with **Luke Raley**, **Josh Smith**, **Dillon Dingler**, **Pedro Ramirez**, **Luis Pena**, and **Jacob Gonzalez** today as they lack the necessary situational upside to justify burning a game cap.",
